Date: Wed, 3 Nov 93 14:42:37 EST
From: david@WAL6000A.UDC.UPENN.EDU (R. David Murray)
Subject: BLINK Interview with Dr. Roger Schank

In my Net wanderings I occasionally find some real gems.

Too bad hypertext links in email don't work smoothly yet.  But here is a
link to an article that I think most of the readers of this list will
find interesting.:

http://www.acns.nwu.edu/blink/issue1/schank.interview.html

Dr. Schank has some fascinating views about education that fit well into
the Extropian memeset.  The interview covers some broad details about
some interesting educational technology he is working on.  To give you a
flavor for the tone, here are the concluding paragraphs:

----------------------------------------------------------------
BLINK: Certainly a large part of improving the system would be played
by developing the technology that you discuss.  But do you see other
things that need to be done in order to move our educational system
toward preferring practical evaluation over standardized testing?

SCHANK: I hope it's possible, but actually I kind of think it's
hopeless.  I think that this society is sick at its roots.  It's just
fascinated with test scores and memorizing irrelevant facts.  I asked
my fall quarter class, "There are a hundred kids here: how many of you
could pass a high school biology test?" Only three thought they could.
Three thought they could pass!  Certainly they must have done OK, or
they wouldn't be at Northwestern in the first place.

What happens is basically you memorize a lot of crap you don't need to
know.  That's what school is about for most people, especially high
school.  It's probably hopeless because the politicians don't want to
change.  Every time you get a new president, they all agree on higher
standards and more tests.  As long as that's the case, there's no
hope.  The only solution I can think of is the computer solution.  The
reason I think it's a solution is that if we specially design the
software so that it doesn't have any of these biases in there, but it
is good, then people will be compelled to take it.

In the meantime, I'm concentrating on business, where we don't have
such problems.
----------------------------------------------------------------

