"Dreams of a final theory".  "Everything interesting has already
been invented".  I don't buy it.  Now, perhaps we've
explained most of, say, motion.  Newton explained most of it,
and Einstein has picked up (most of) the rest.  But on the other
hand, perhaps there are very interesting needles in the search space
that we haven't found yet (a mathematics that can describe "chaotic"
processes more predictably, wormholes, temporal communication,
quantum-mechanical teleportation on a macro scale -- all quite 
speculative, of course, and I'm not saying any of those specific
examples are likely, but breakthroughs of this nature, or more
likely of a currently unimagined nature but equally sweeping,
cannot be ruled out).

> Perhaps even completely
> rewriting our interpretation of current theories. But there are few
> things out there now that defy interpretation, at least not in the way
> that a century ago there were some obvious phenomena that had no real
> explanation.

Examples?  I'd say the GRO bursters, cold "fusion", the solar neutrino
deficit, etc. are major holes that might shoot right to the core
of physical theory.  For example, how do you explain the excess
heat in the cold "fusion" experiments?  Completely baffling.  And there 
is still a vast amount to explore in quantum phenomenon: for example
is Heisenberg uncertainty very chaotic or completely random?  There
are libraries full of other skeletons lurking in the closet of
modern physics.  In any case, comparing this to unexplained (not
stuff explained incorrectly without widespread knowledge of the
error, mind you, but truly unexplained) phenomenon at the beginning
of the century will be a hazardous chore.  Radioactivity?  Was
radioactivity even considered important until the X-ray was
invented?

> (And the technologies our grandparents saw
> introduced--light bulbs, cars, airplanes, penicillin, radios,
> television, etc.--pretty much dwarfed anything I can recall seeing
> introduced in my lifetime, except of course for the computer and DNA and
> perhaps things like lasers).

Our grandparents have seen twice as many years!  Furthermore, it's
far easier to say with hindsight what was revolutionary in 1930 than
what is revolutionary to day.  Your list spans a period ranging 
from the 1870s (light bulb and automobile) to 1940s (television).  A similar 
70-year period from 1920 to 1990 gave us:

Plastics -- have greatly improved the construction of a vast variety
	of materials (packaging, boxes, surfaces, on and on)
Wide varities of cleaners, glues, tape, post-it notes, whiteboards, etc.
	(many other things made possible by modern chemistry)
computers, calculators, etc. (a wide variety of forms, worth
	more than radio & TV combined in my estimation) w/
	vast variety of software (operating systems, databases,
	spreadsheets, high-level languages, compilers, networks,
	e-mail, cryptography, etc. etc.)
ATMs, bar-code scanners
cellular phones, Walkmans, VCRs, CDs, video games, etc. etc.
nuclear fission: powerplants, bombs, etc. (more revolutionary
	than any weapons tech during 1870-1940)
tanks, jet planes, missiles, drone reconassaince, radar, etc.
	(20th century for the developed world was quite violent 
	compared to the 19th, so no surprise much of the energy wa
	channelled here).
space flight, spysats, comsats, astronauts on Moon, spacecraft 
	to all planets except Pluto, etc.
birth control pill (in many ways more revolutionary than any other
	technology in recent centuries)
artificial organs & organ transplants
in-vitro fertilization
monoclonal antibodies
PCR
genetic engineering
nuclear medicine 
CAT scanners
cryonics (dwarfs pennicillin et. al, but not yet "mainstream")

I'm sure I've missed quite a few!  Any one of nuclear power, Von Neumann 
computers, and the birth control pill alone dwarf anything that came 
in the previous 70 years.

Alas, our judgements are quite qualitiative.  Pessimists can go on being
pessimists, and optimists optimists, without any objective reputation.
That's why I'd like to find more general measures such as the
computational complexity of a civilization (cf. my previous
post on this thread).

> - a theory of computation (Turing, Post, Church, Godel)

But we had Von Neumann computers and language theory in the
1940s, Kolmogorov complexity in the 1960s, a massive exploration 
of computational complexity (NSPACE, PSPACE, etc.) which probably 
dwarfs what Church & Turing did.  Major forays into artificial
life being made right now (Tom Ray's work, genetic algorithms, etc.
etc.)  Again, it's much harder to tell which of today's theories
will be as important as Church & Turing's: we've got enough
experience to know they were important and put them in a textbook.
30 years ago, Turing's work was as difficult to find as Kolmogorov's
is today.   Another phenomenon: we are swamped by _so much_ new
theory that the most important stuff takes longer to percolate out.
Just compare volume: we dwarf previous era's theoretical work by
orders of magnitude.  Perhaps more of it is garbage, but not 
enough more of it to make up for the volume.

Tim, your theory implies a huge degradation in the human intellect
during the course of the twentieth century, specifically it implies
that the garbage/content ratio has increased by more than an order of
magnitude.   To what cause(s) do you attribute this incredible disaster,
and what remedies can you suggest?

> Here's what I think we have seen:
>    
> "rate of science"
>       ^                            
>       |              
>       |           **
>       |          *   *  
>       |         *        *   ?
>       |        * 
>       |       *
>       |     *
>       |   *
>       |*
>       |_____________________> time
>      1800      1900     2000


This looks exagerrated (of course there is no scale).  Modern
biology vastly outstrips that of the first half of the century.
For physics, I'd put the peak in the 1930's (quantum mechanics, GR, etc.)
Between the Holocaust (which killed half of the population
that produced most of the top 30s-era physcists) and the
conversion of physicists into glorified engineers and bureaucrats
working for DOE, NSF et. al. (reaching the nadir in $11 billion
collider to find one particle), it's no surprise that physics
has slowed down.  We've got so much unexplained data now that
there is huge potential for a new breath of life, from a
new generation of physicists who go into it because they
like physics, not because there's money/power in it, combined with
the new computational tools (which will soon be able to do 90%+ of 
the visible work done by previous generations of physicists, eg most
of the math, model-fitting, etc., and do it in regimes of complexity
unimaginable to Einstein et. al.)

>[specious comparison of GP with theorem-provers]

A much better comparison is with the modern statistical analysis packages, 
now becoming ubiquitous in science labs.  GP's most familiar mode 
of operation is a generalization of regression, automating most of the 
decision as to what kind of model to fit.  No scientist has yet even 
tried GA or GP for this purpose; as of a year ago only three people on 
the planet were studying GP, and as I've noted the major focus so far is 
on robotics, control, market prediction, and the like, not science.  But 
the fact of the matter is that GP can do the work that takes up the bulk 
of scientists time, ie generating mathematical theories that best fit the 
data & comparing them to current theory, and in many regimes experiments 
to verify/falsify them, much more quickly than is 
possible for a human.  (Of course, we can redefine "science" as being 
whatever mystical "conceptualization" that the GP can't do.  Fine.  
The fact remains, GP can bring orders-of-magnitude improvement to those 
apsects of science as it was defined before GP entered the discussion,
leaving scientists free to do the rest, much of that also with
major computerized improvement of other sorts, eg on-line
communications, typesetting, etc.)

> but any talk of counting on the doublings of
> computer power every few years to solve some presently intractable
> problem probably is already going down the wrong path.

There's a fundamental difference in my argument.  I'm talking about 
computing power combined with known algorithms, not some speculative,
unspecified "AI breakthrough" or "knowledge of how humans do
it".  I'm not contending GP can mimic humans; I'm contending that
combined with other computational tools they can do most
most of the work that goes into science, in most cases many
of orders of magnitude more quickly than humans can, and beyond
that at a speed that continues to double as computational speeds   
double. (And the doubling time itself might shrink, as silicon
compiler technology matures and quantum phenomenon are more
quickly discovered and incorporated into the latest generation
of chip).

