Perhaps it's small.  Do the physicists out there have some good
esitimates for this?

> > Sumerians+Egyptians: 100 priests*1 IPS = 100 IPS
> 
> Well, this is kind of misleading I think.  It implies that
> my own PC (10 MIPS, conservatively) should be able to recapitulate
> 1000 years of ancient scientific discovery in just a few days.

With GP and good data, it well might take a big chunk out of those
1,000 years.  In its sole foray into science
so far,  GP recapitulated Kepler's laws, much more advanced than 
anything the Egyptians came up with in astronomy, in less than a day.
And GP is far from the only tool available.  The math known to the 
Egyptians is child's play for Mathematica.  Heck, most of the math 
known before the 20th century is child's play for Mathematica.

> conceptual innovation

This is sheer mysticism.  Scientists aren't performing magic,
they are going through a process, most of which can now be
specified by algorithms, eg those in GP, Mathematica, 
statistical analysis packages, etc. 

> The largest challenge, I believe, is one of representation. 

GP solves this problem, by evolving the representation that
best fits data and theory.  This in contrast to current
statistical packages which have a wide variety of narrow
options for the kind of model to try to fit, don't build
on or check for consistency with previous theories, etc.

> Here's a challenge for you, Nick:  Pick a single GP-style
> experiment you forsee as promising, and we can try to imagine
> the GP system that could grind away on it.

We should pick an area where (a) data is available, preferably
on the Internet, and (b) that is familiar to the physicists
