Of course not! People can be, and are, just as smart as before. But
the late 19th and early 20th century happened (we could debated the
economic and sociological reasons, the links with markets, the
expansion of industry, etc.) to be a time of major exploration of
previously unexplored parts 
of knowledge space. This gave us the
theories of evolution and genetics (Darwin, genetics), quantum
mechanics (Plankc, Bohr, Heisenberg, Schrodinger, Dirac, etc.),
relativity (Einstein), modern communications theory (Nyquist,
Shannon), modern computer theory (Turing, Godel, etc.), and on and on.


> >[specious comparison of GP with theorem-provers]

Now Nick, let's not let this interesting debate degenerate.

In fact, I'll wager 5000 pounds....never mind. ;-}

I'm all for using the power of computers, but I don't think there's
much chance the rate of major unifying theories is going to
be dramatically increased by GP or any other system I've seen.

I think it was Rutherford who said "All science is either physics or
stamp collecting." While overly dismissive, and probably not even
fully believed by him, there's a kernel of truth there. 

The "basic theories" are generally the extreme compressions of data: F
= ma, gravity, chemical theory, Uncertainty Principle, etc. In AIT
terms, there are not many "short programs" that describe
nature...there can't be, by counting arguments.

In "non-basic" theories, complexity can grow, as species of animals
and plants are discovered, as geologic formations are identified and
mapped, as new compounds are synthesized, as new widgets and gizmos
are built, and so on. These are important, for technological and
economic reasons, and even for pushing out into new regions of the
"knowledge mine."

My contention is that right now we are mainly exploring the "stamp
collecting" part of the mine, which is probably what we _should_ be
exploring and building upon.

This doesn't reflect "degradation of human intellect," to use Nick's
incorrect characterization of my point, but it does reflect the fact
that the "easy pickings" in terms of fundamental science have been
picked.

How much new "science" emerges--and I don't just mean physics--remains
to be seen. Certainly a lot will...my curve just "turned-over"--it
didn't drop precipitiously.

Interestingly, as the number of dimensions of knowledge space
increases, the overall radial expansion can slow and still allow the
hypervolume of knowledge to rise exponentially, which is another way
of seeing my point. (I have some MORE-based outlines of this model,
which I may clean up someday into essay form.)


--Tim May


