Subject: biology

The science discussion has reminded me of a series of articles I've
been running into lately in various publications, culminating in a
fascinating article I read in the most recent Scientific American on
the precise mechanism by which several neurotransmitters function --
by "precise" I mean down to "the receptor is a micromachine that
changes its conformation like this when the transmitter attaches, and
then allows ions to flow through like this" level. Recent articles in
Science News discussed the use of tracking agents to observe with
great precision the mechanisms by which various processes occur in
cells. The degree to which the "final secrets" of biology have started
to be extracted is astonishing. Its certainly possible that within
thirty to seventy years an exact explanation for all biological
processes will be available, just in time for nanotechnology,
incidently. The end of current biology as an interesting discipline
(in the same sense that basic optics or mechanics ceased to be an
interesting discipline) is at hand -- within striking distance. I find
this to be an astonishing, and strangely unheralded, development.

