Date: Wed, 3 Nov 93 16:53:51 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: META: The ::Exclusion Principle

I've been meaning to write a small piece on the hazards of the
::Exclusion Principle, you know, that thing St. Pauli Girl invented
after a few mugs too many.

One of the dangers when many people are ::excluding threads, or
authors, or are just plain not reading all messsages is that the same
news keeps appearing over and over again.

For example, this tidbit: (the author is not cited, as my point is not
to single him out)
> 
> I found this in the Nov 1 New York Times:
> 
> "On Oct 18 employees of Panix Public Access found that an
> intruder had secretly inserted a rouge program into one of their
> computers. The program was designed to watch network data
> communications and to record password information in a secret
> file." 

Now perhaps he did not see the earlier mentions by others, or the
discussion by Harry and Company when the list was coming back up. Or
perhaps he felt the news items needed repetition, to make sure we got
it.

And he may be that "::exclude" had nothing to do with it. After all, I
saw the same item repeated a couple of times on the Cypherpunks list.

But it relates to the points Hal Finney brought up several days ago:
lists like ours always run the risk of turning into a bunch of people
announcing what they've just read or seen, what they feel about
certain current issues, and other ephemera. They may be oblivious to
points made by ohters, for whatever reasons.

The wider use of sophisticated ::exclude agents will worsen the
situation and could, paradoxically, increase the noise-to-signal ratio
further. (Yes, ordinary newsgroups have this problem, too, but at
least with newsgroups there are several thousand topic-oriented areas,
so not everyone sees out-of-band announcements).

As Harry noted, a few of us try to quickly scan nearly every article
that appears here. Thus, my own "meat agent" will know, for example,
that the Panix hacking situation has already been reported and I will
not be tempted to post my own item on it. (I cannot see any automated
agent able to screen like this anytime soon.)

Something for us to think about.

--Tim May


