Charlie Stross writes:
> more-capitalist-than-capitalist, having the goal of abolishing
> everything except the market -- so that all can live in a state of
> primal acquisitive freedom.  My gut feeling is that both
 
  Anarchocapitalism does not seek to abolish all organizations except the
market. Anarchocapitalism seeks to abolish coercive organizations. A true
anarchocapitalist should have no quarrel with labor unions, the united
way, collectivist communes, etc as long as there were no state enforcing them.
(i.e. no "can't hire permanent replacement workers" laws)
  It is not organizations and cooperation we wish to abolish, it is 
initiation of coercion by organizations (property rights taken as
an axiom)

> It's probably not overstating things to say that a modern, developed
> society is so complex that nobody can handle _all_ their personal
> interactions adequately on their own.  I pay an accountant to file my
> tax returns; I pay an insurance broker to insure me; I 'pay'
> (compulsorily) for the government's security service (the Police) to
> protect me.  It makes sense to pay other people to do things I _could_
> in principle do for myself -- because there aren't enough hours in the
> week for me to do all of them.

  Exactly. You get to _choose_ to pay someone to do labor you don't
want to do or for services you don't want to provide. This is division
of labor, an inherent byproduct of a market.

> The market is of course a very efficient mechanism for mediating all
> sorts of social exchange, including the provision of basic services.
> But it's still a mediational mechanism that takes some of the input
> resources just to keep it running.  The idea the socialists had was to
> replace it with something more efficient.  Trouble was, their idea of a
> more efficient system turned out to be less efficient under most

  I oppose socialism on both utilitarian and moral grounds. Utilitarian
because it simply doesn't work, has failed everytime it's been tried, and
generally devolves any society it fails on. I oppose it on moral grounds
simply because I don't want the state deciding what I can and can't do
with my life and my property. Even if there was some theoretical
socialist algorithm which was more efficient than the market, I would
still oppose it because such an algorithm would need to control me, otherwise
it wouldn't have complete information on my desires. 

  Even if it did, Tim May recently posted an article from bionet.info-theory
which shows that not all determinisic systems are predictable, therefore,
it is completly possible that there is _NO_ algorithmic predictable reduction
of the market (e.g. the market is incompressible). If the market's success is
related to its complexity, a socialist algorithm would need to be even
more complex (all other things being equal, like people's desires, expectations
of personal freedom) and probably just as unpredictable.

> better mousetrap is invalid.  Find a resource-distribution algorithm
> that's faster or has lower overheads than the market algorithm, and that
> can work in non-linear systems, and what have you got?

  A centralized totalitarian economy with human beings treated as inputs
to a computer program?

> Question: is the complexity of society increasing on a similar
> exponential curve to our information processing capability? If so, is
> the information-processing ability of the market going 
> to keep pace with the social system it's mediating? Go figure ...

   I would say yes, since the market has feedback loops. If someone
were to invent a super information processing system which could
always beat the market, then everyone could use it, negating its effect. As
long as most people have around the same level of information processing
capability, the market survives.  (if not, then those with significantly 
greater processing power beat the market, get rich, and head off into the
singularity)

   Also, capitalism tends to prevent such concentration of power over
long periods of time. A capitalist who invented such an automated trader
would probably not keep it secret, rather he would sell it to lots of
other traders for megabux.


