Date: Wed, 3 Nov 93 18:55:27 PST
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Future Science

Tim May: 
> Well, of course I made no such claims. I said "slowing down," not
> "final theory," etc.

A mere semantic quibble.   You claimed we've exhausted 80% of the
physics search space, which amounts to approaching a "final theory"
As my list showed, at least the technological side of things is 
greatly accelerating.  My points about the difficulty of determining 
the consequences of more recent theories, etc. still stand, of course.  
What you are seeing is a difference in our ability to perceive the
consequences of things (ie the importance of a theory), not
a difference in the rate at which theories are being found.
Nor, indeed, have you refuted any of my speculative suggestions
on new areas basic physics might explore (and indeed, is exploring --
temporal communication, wormholes, etc. are all areas of active
interest by top-flight physicists like Kip Thorne). Now, I happen
to find the cliched ideas like time travel less probable than
novel directions, because more of the search space of the former
has been explored without luck.  (My "Law of the Dominant Paradigm").
But the existence of such research, and the many unexplained phenomenon,
show physics in ferment, not a science that has exhausted 80% of
its possibilities.  Of course, it would help if more physicists
were pursuing such ideas and less lobbying for $11 billion pork
projects or becoming glorified engineers for same.

One of the lessons of evolution is "punctuated equilibrium" --
periods of stasis or slow growth punctuated by periods of ferment
as pathways to new fertile areas are opened.  Even if we've entered
a period of stasis, that doesn't tell us that we've explored most
of the search space.

> As to the other things, let me again state that I never claimed
> science has ended, that nothing new remains to be done. I argued about
> rates, so anecdotal citations of things still needing explanation

No, you argued that there is less that needs to be explained now than
at the turn of the century.  I argue that the opposite is true.
Of course statistics would be better than anecdotes, but are hard
to come by.

> Of course not! People can be, and are, just as smart as before. But
> the late 19th and early 20th century happened (we could debated the
> economic and sociological reasons, the links with markets, the
> expansion of industry, etc.) to be a time of major exploration of
> previously unexplored parts  of knowledge space. 

Let's debate them.  Why 19th and early 20th?  Why not 18th or 
10th or 3rd BC?  Why not mid and late 20th?  Why not 21st century?  

> theories of evolution and genetics (Darwin, genetics), quantum
> mechanics (Plankc, Bohr, Heisenberg, Schrodinger, Dirac, etc.),
> relativity (Einstein), modern communications theory (Nyquist,
> Shannon), modern computer theory (Turing, Godel, etc.), and on and on.

"There you go again!".  This covers 110 years, from 1840s (Darwin) to 1940s
(communications & computer theory), compared to 50 years of
theory since then, the consequences of which are harder to determine
because they mostly haven't happened yet.  That we can name more
important theories in the longer, older period doesn't mean things
have slowed down.  I previously listed a boatload of theory 
developed since 1940; again of course we're comparing anecdotal evidence.

> I think it was Rutherford who said "All science is either physics or
> stamp collecting." While overly dismissive, and probably not even
> fully believed by him, there's a kernel of truth there. 

Computers are superb at stamp collecting, and will soon be quite
good at physics.  Already they're very good at doing the math
of physics, simulations, statistical regressions, etc. if set up
properly; GP adds the capability of discovering new models.
In most cases software is orders of magnitude faster than
human scientists, and can take advantage of the ongoing increases
in computer speed.

> The "basic theories" are generally the extreme compressions of data:

And indeed, GP is quite good at compression.  (In fact, there's a 
company in Palo Alto now using GP to design compression algorithms,
a kind of meta-compression).

> there are not many "short programs" that describe
> nature...there can't be, by counting arguments.

Well, let's count them.  Take an alphabet of 52 symbols (really
larger -- upper & lower case, strange fonts, Greek symbols,
etc. -- science struggles and strains to increase its alphabet
size).  The number of possible 100-symbol theories is then
52^100 = 10^170.  Vastly larger than if we turned the entire
planetary crust into microfilmed scientific theories, but 
for most topologies this search space is a piece of cake for
GP.

> This doesn't reflect "degradation of human intellect," to use Nick's
> incorrect characterization of my point, but it does reflect the fact
> that the "easy pickings" in terms of fundamental science have been
> picked.

But you haven't explained why most of the "easy pickings" occured in one
era but not the previous or the next.  I don't buy it.  I can buy
superiority over the previous era because of better instruments,
spread of literacy, etc. -- but those trends continue apace, the
recent progress in instruments (NMR, STM, large telescopes, data
processing, etc. etc.) is unprecedented, the aquisition of new data on 
new phenomenon continues to accelerate (sans considerations of the 
bureaucratization of science as in the space program, which I've 
discussed as a possible reason for temporary slowdown since the 1940s,
unrelated to the topology of the search space).

> Interestingly, as the number of dimensions of knowledge space
> increases, the overall radial expansion can slow and still allow the
> hypervolume of knowledge to rise exponentially, which is another way
> of seeing my point. 

I would like to hear more about this!  But if the number of dimensions
are increasing, it doesn't make sense to focus on the slowdown in a few
dimensions and claim that "science is slowing down".

